      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Smart Search Skill Entry Point \u003c-- skills/code_smart_search/main.go:87\n├── run() main skill logic \u003c-- 1a\n│   ├── generateCandidates() \u003c-- 1b\n│   │   ├── NewGenerator() creation \u003c-- 1c\n│   │   │   └── store + workspace setup \u003c-- skills/code_smart_search/main.go:181\n│   │   └── generator.Generate() call \u003c-- 1d\n│   │       ├── Symbol index search \u003c-- internal/retrieval/candidates.go:121\n│   │       ├── Semantic vector search \u003c-- internal/retrieval/candidates.go:133\n│   │       └── Ripgrep fallback \u003c-- internal/retrieval/candidates.go:150\n│   └── invokeSnippetExtract() call \u003c-- 1e\n│       ├── Build extract input JSON \u003c-- skills/code_smart_search/main.go:251\n│       └── Execute snippet_extract skill \u003c-- skills/code_smart_search/main.go:266\n└── skillmain.Main() wrapper \u003c-- skills/code_smart_search/main.go:87\n    └── Command registration \u003c-- skills/code_smart_search/main.go:28",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen developers search code, they need more than simple text matching - they need to find **relevant code blocks** that actually solve their problem. The code/smart_search skill solves this by combining intelligent candidate generation from multiple sources (symbol index, semantic embeddings, and ripgrep fallback) with precise snippet extraction, giving developers the most relevant code without manual hunting.\n\n## Details\n\nThe smart search pipeline starts with the main entry point [1a] which orchestrates two key phases. First, it generates ranked file candidates using the retrieval package's Generator [1b]. This generator creates a memory store connection [1c] and calls Generate() [1d] to search three sources in parallel: the symbol index for function/class names, semantic embeddings for conceptual similarity, and ripgrep as a fallback. The candidates are then merged and ranked with weighted scoring.\n\nSecond, it invokes the snippet_extract skill [1e] with the generated candidates. This builds a JSON payload [1e] and executes the skill as a subprocess [1e], which reads the actual files, extracts relevant code blocks using keyword matching and symbol-aware parsing, and returns the final snippets. The results are aggregated with statistics about candidates generated and snippets extracted.\n\nThe beauty of this design is that each skill has a **single responsibility**: smart_search orchestrates, retrieval generates candidates, and snippet_extract does the heavy lifting of file processing and code block extraction."
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "title": "Retrieval Generation Orchestration",
      "description": "Core retrieval package that combines multiple search sources and ranks candidates",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "2a",
          "path": "internal/retrieval/candidates.go",
          "lineNumber": 111,
          "lineContent": "func (g *Generator) Generate(ctx context.Context, workspaceID, question string, opts Options) (*GenerateResult, error) {",
          "title": "Generate Method Entry",
          "description": "Main orchestration method for candidate generation"
        },
        {
          "id": "2b",
          "path": "internal/retrieval/candidates.go",
          "lineNumber": 121,
          "lineContent": "candidates, err := g.searchSymbolIndex(ctx, workspaceID, question, opts.MaxSymbolCandidates)",
          "title": "Symbol Index Search",
          "description": "Searches indexed code symbols for matches"
        },
        {
          "id": "2c",
          "path": "internal/retrieval/candidates.go",
          "lineNumber": 133,
          "lineContent": "candidates, err := g.searchSemanticIndex(ctx, workspaceID, question, opts.MaxSemanticCandidates)",
          "title": "Semantic Vector Search",
          "description": "Performs embedding-based similarity search"
        },
        {
          "id": "2d",
          "path": "internal/retrieval/candidates.go",
          "lineNumber": 150,
          "lineContent": "candidates, err := g.ripgrepFallback(ctx, question, opts.MaxRipgrepCandidates)",
          "title": "Ripgrep Fallback",
          "description": "Uses ripgrep when symbol/semantic results are sparse"
        },
        {
          "id": "2e",
          "path": "internal/retrieval/candidates.go",
          "lineNumber": 168,
          "lineContent": "merged := mergeCandidates(allCandidates, weights, opts.MaxTotalCandidates)",
          "title": "Merge and Rank",
          "description": "Combines candidates from all sources with weighted scoring"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Retrieval Generation Orchestration\n├── Generate() main entry point \u003c-- 2a\n│   ├── Initialize allCandidates \u0026 stats \u003c-- internal/retrieval/candidates.go:116\n│   ├── searchSymbolIndex() \u003c-- 2b\n│   │   └── Build search terms from question \u003c-- internal/retrieval/symbol_search.go:19\n│   ├── searchSemanticIndex() \u003c-- 2c\n│   │   ├── Generate query embedding \u003c-- internal/retrieval/semantic_search.go:29\n│   │   └── Execute hybrid search \u003c-- internal/retrieval/semantic_search.go:39\n│   ├── ripgrepFallback() \u003c-- 2d\n│   │   └── Extract keywords \u0026 run rg \u003c-- internal/retrieval/ripgrep.go:20\n│   └── mergeCandidates() \u003c-- 2e\n│       ├── Group by file path \u003c-- internal/retrieval/merge.go:22\n│       ├── Apply source weights \u003c-- internal/retrieval/merge.go:31\n│       └── Sort \u0026 limit results \u003c-- internal/retrieval/merge.go:61\n└── Return GenerateResult with candidates \u003c-- internal/retrieval/candidates.go:185",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen you search for code in a large codebase, you need to find the **most relevant files** quickly. Simple text search returns too many irrelevant results, while semantic search alone might miss exact symbol matches. The retrieval system solves this by **combining three complementary search methods**: symbol index search (finds exact function/class names), semantic vector search (finds conceptually similar code), and ripgrep fallback (catches anything missed by the indexes). This hybrid approach ensures you get the best possible candidates regardless of how you phrase your search query.\n\n## Details\n\nThe retrieval pipeline starts in `Generate()` [2a] which orchestrates three parallel searches. First, `searchSymbolIndex()` [2b] queries the symbol index using tokenized search terms, scoring matches based on symbol names, signatures, and documentation. Second, `searchSemanticIndex()` [2c] converts your question to an embedding vector and performs hybrid BM25+vector similarity search. Third, if the first two methods return few results, `ripgrepFallback()` [2d] extracts keywords from your query and runs ripgrep to find files containing those terms.\n\nAll candidates flow into `mergeCandidates()` [2e] which groups by file path, applies source weights (symbol search gets weight 1.0, semantic 0.7, ripgrep 0.5), and gives a **multi-source bonus** when the same file appears from multiple sources. The final ranked list is returned as `GenerateResult` with detailed statistics about how many candidates came from each source.\n\nThis design ensures **graceful degradation** - if symbol indexes are missing, you still get semantic results; if embeddings fail, you get BM25 and ripgrep; if everything fails, ripgrep still provides basic text search coverage."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "title": "Semantic Search Implementation",
      "description": "Vector-based semantic search using embedding providers and hybrid BM25+vector search",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "3a",
          "path": "internal/retrieval/semantic_search.go",
          "lineNumber": 14,
          "lineContent": "func (g *Generator) searchSemanticIndex(ctx context.Context, workspaceID, question string, limit int) ([]Candidate, error) {",
          "title": "Semantic Search Entry",
          "description": "Main semantic search implementation"
        },
        {
          "id": "3b",
          "path": "internal/retrieval/semantic_search.go",
          "lineNumber": 29,
          "lineContent": "vec, err := g.embedProvider.Embed(ctx, question)",
          "title": "Generate Query Embedding",
          "description": "Converts question to vector using embedding provider"
        },
        {
          "id": "3c",
          "path": "internal/retrieval/semantic_search.go",
          "lineNumber": 39,
          "lineContent": "results, err := searchable.SearchHybrid(ctx, question, queryVec, workspaceID, limit*2)",
          "title": "Hybrid Search Execution",
          "description": "Performs BM25 + vector similarity search"
        },
        {
          "id": "3d",
          "path": "internal/retrieval/semantic_search.go",
          "lineNumber": 50,
          "lineContent": "return g.memoryResultsToCandidates(results, limit)",
          "title": "Convert to Candidates",
          "description": "Transforms memory search results to candidate objects"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Semantic Search Implementation\n├── searchSemanticIndex() entry point \u003c-- 3a\n│   ├── Check embedding provider availability \u003c-- internal/retrieval/semantic_search.go:15\n│   ├── Generate query embedding \u003c-- 3b\n│   │   └── embedProvider.Embed(ctx, question) \u003c-- internal/retrieval/semantic_search.go:29\n│   ├── Perform hybrid search \u003c-- 3c\n│   │   ├── searchable.SearchHybrid() \u003c-- internal/retrieval/semantic_search.go:39\n│   │   └── Fallback to BM25 if needed \u003c-- internal/retrieval/semantic_search.go:41\n│   └── Convert results to candidates \u003c-- 3d\n│       ├── memoryResultsToCandidates() \u003c-- internal/retrieval/semantic_search.go:50\n│       └── Extract file paths from entries \u003c-- internal/retrieval/semantic_search.go:143\n└── BM25 fallback path \u003c-- internal/retrieval/semantic_search.go:54\n    ├── store.Search() simple search \u003c-- internal/retrieval/semantic_search.go:56\n    └── Convert to candidates with scoring \u003c-- internal/retrieval/semantic_search.go:84",
      "traceGuide": "# Motivation\n\nSemantic search solves the **fundamental problem** of finding code based on meaning rather than exact keywords. Traditional text search fails when you don't know the exact function names or variable names used in a codebase. By converting both code and queries into vector embeddings, the system can find semantically similar files even when they use different terminology - like finding \"authentication logic\" when the code uses \"login validation\" or \"user verification\" terminology.\n\n# Details\n\nThe semantic search system operates as a **hybrid search engine** that combines vector similarity with traditional BM25 text search. When a user submits a query, the system first generates an embedding vector using providers like Voyage or Gemini APIs [3b]. This vector is then compared against pre-indexed file embeddings using either pure vector similarity or hybrid search that also considers text matches [3c].\n\nThe system gracefully **degrades to BM25-only search** when embedding providers are unavailable or when vector search fails [3a]. This ensures the search remains functional even without API keys or during network issues. Results from both search modes are converted into standardized candidate objects that include file paths, relevance scores, and metadata [3d].\n\nA key innovation is the **file path extraction logic** that parses different memory entry formats (symbol://, file://, semantic://) to consistently extract workspace-relative file paths for candidate generation. This allows the same search infrastructure to work across different indexing strategies while maintaining a unified candidate format."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "title": "Snippet Extraction Process",
      "description": "Extracts relevant code blocks from candidate files using keyword matching and symbol-aware extraction",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "4a",
          "path": "skills/code_snippet_extract/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 169,
          "lineContent": "func run(ctx context.Context, rc *skillmain.RunContext, in Input) error {",
          "title": "Snippet Extract Entry",
          "description": "Main skill logic for extracting code snippets"
        },
        {
          "id": "4b",
